<ArticleBody depth={3}>

<p>When two requests change the same data at the same time, each can be correct on its own and the result can still be wrong. This article shows how to keep a business rule true when requests overlap, and when one of them fails part-way through. It builds on <a href={`${next}#a-transaction-protects-a-change-within-a-stated-contract`}>the introduction’s section on transactions</a>, where two customers race for the shop’s last mug, but it explains each term as it comes up, so you can start here.</p>
<p>In short, a transaction makes a group of changes commit together or not at all. It does not stop another request from acting on data you have just read, so the decision needs protecting as well as the write. Often one statement can do both, such as “take a place only if one is left”. A constraint can refuse a bad state outright. If a rule depends on several rows, either make every writer lock the same row first, or run every writer at the Serializable level, which refuses one of two conflicting transactions so that it can be run again. Any of these protections can end a transaction with an error. The application must then rerun the whole operation, not just the statement that failed. And when the reply to a commit is lost, the application has to find out what happened through the operation’s own identity.</p>
<p>The shop from the introduction also runs classes, which give us a few more rules to protect. Mina Shah, Tariq Aziz and Edda Berg are customers. <code>classes.remaining</code> stores the places still free in each class, and each row in <code>reservations</code> is one booking, identified by the request that made it. Class 21, Tea tasting, has one place and no bookings. Class 22, Mug painting, has three places, and Tariq already holds one through request R7. Two instructors, Lena and Ravi, teach class 21. Each request runs in its own <strong>session</strong>, one connection’s conversation with the database.</p>

