<p>PostgreSQL 18 is the reference engine. MySQL’s InnoDB and SQLite appear where their behaviour changes what you should write. The five numbered figures replay runs recorded on PostgreSQL 18.6: each step shows a statement, what the database returned, and the committed rows afterwards. <a href="#where-the-results-come-from">Where the results come from</a>, near the end, says how they were recorded.</p>

<h2 id="start-with-the-fact-that-must-remain-true">Invariants</h2>
<p>An <strong>invariant</strong> is a statement about the committed data that must hold after every commit. Before choosing any mechanism, write the invariants down. The class bookings have three:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Places add up.</strong> For each class, <code>remaining</code> plus the number of its bookings equals its <code>capacity</code>. Taking a place and recording the booking always happen together.</li>
  <li><strong>One booking per customer per class.</strong> Mina can’t hold two places in class 22.</li>
  <li><strong>Class 21 keeps a teacher.</strong> At least one of its instructors is available.</li>
</ol>
<p>The rules have different shapes, and the shape decides which protections can work. The first ties a class row to a count of rows in another table, but every booking also changes that class row, so that row is where competing bookings meet. The second is about pairs of values in one table, which a unique constraint can state directly. The third is about a set of rows, and two changes can each touch a different row of the set without ever touching the same one.</p>
<p>Next, list the outcomes you would accept. Suppose Mina and Tariq both ask for class 21’s one place. Either Mina gets it and Tariq is told the class is full, or the other way round. Both booked is wrong, and so is neither booked with <code>remaining</code> at 0. That list is the test every later protection has to pass.</p>
<p>It also separates two kinds of fault. A booking that inserts a reservation but forgets to reduce <code>remaining</code> is wrong even when it runs alone, and an ordinary test finds it. A booking that reads <code>remaining</code>, checks it is above zero and then writes the new value is correct alone. It fails only when two run at the same moment, which is why single-user testing never shows it. Most of this article is about the second kind.</p>
<p>The schema already states the rules a database can check by itself. <code>CHECK (remaining BETWEEN 0 AND capacity)</code> stops a class going below zero or above capacity, <code>UNIQUE (class_id, owner_id)</code> is rule 2, and the primary key on <code>request_id</code> means the same request can be recorded only once.</p>
<Sql code={schemaSql} label="The class-booking tables, with the rules the database can check" />

<h3 id="put-the-complete-change-inside-a-boundary">Transactions, autocommit and ACID</h3>
<p>A booking is two changes: take a place in <code>classes</code>, and record the booking in <code>reservations</code>. If the application sends them without <code>BEGIN</code>, PostgreSQL runs each statement as a transaction of its own and commits it as soon as it finishes. This is called <strong>autocommit</strong>, and it is what most drivers do unless asked otherwise.</p>
<p>When PostgreSQL refuses a statement, it returns an error with a five-character code, and this article names errors by those codes. The first one you will meet is 23505: a value that must be unique already exists. Figure 1 follows Mina double-clicking Book in class 22, which sends two requests, R10 and R11, one after the other. Play it with <em>Each statement on its own</em>, then switch to <em>One transaction per booking</em> and watch the committed rows when R11’s insert is refused with 23505.</p>
<ScheduleFigure figure="clicks" number={1} caption="One session. With separate statements, R11’s place stays taken after its insert is refused, so class 22 has 0 remaining and 2 bookings against a capacity of 3. Inside one transaction the refusal undoes R11’s place." />
<p>With separate statements, nothing undid R11’s first change, and a place disappeared. Inside a transaction, the refused insert left the whole transaction failed. PostgreSQL then refuses every further statement with 25P02 until the transaction ends, and a <code>COMMIT</code> sent to a failed transaction is reported back as <code>ROLLBACK</code>. An application should read that result rather than assume the commit worked.</p>
<p>The three commands are short. <code>BEGIN</code> starts a transaction. <code>COMMIT</code> asks the database to make all its changes permanent and visible together. <code>ROLLBACK</code> discards them all. The guarantees are often summarised as ACID, and it helps to be precise about each letter:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Atomicity</strong> is what Figure 1 showed: all of a transaction’s changes commit, or none do.</li>
  <li><strong>Consistency</strong> means each commit takes the data from one state that meets the declared rules to another. The database can only enforce the rules it knows about. It knows the <code>CHECK</code> and the unique constraint; it does not know rule 1 or rule 3, which hold only if every transaction is written to keep them.</li>
  <li><strong>Isolation</strong> describes how much overlapping transactions can affect each other. The next sections are about it.</li>
  <li><strong>Durability</strong> means a committed change survives a crash, under the conditions the server is configured for. It is covered near the end.</li>
</ul>
<p>A <strong>savepoint</strong> marks a point inside a transaction that you can roll back to without abandoning the rest. It is not a commit: nothing inside it is permanent until the outer transaction commits. Savepoints also make it easy to undo too little. Set one after R11 has taken its place and before its insert, roll back to it when the insert is refused, then commit, and the place stays taken with no booking: Figure 1’s broken state again.</p>
<p>A rollback undoes a transaction’s changes to data, but not quite everything. A sequence, PostgreSQL’s generator of IDs, <a href={`${pg}functions-sequence.html`}>never takes back</a> a number handed out by <code>nextval</code>. A rolled-back transaction that took 1 leaves the next caller with 2, so gaps in generated IDs are normal. In MySQL, most schema changes <a href={`${mysql}implicit-commit.html`}>commit implicitly</a>, so they can’t be rolled back at all, while PostgreSQL rolls back a <code>CREATE TABLE</code> with the rest of its transaction.</p>
<p>How much an error undoes also differs. In PostgreSQL any error fails the whole transaction. In InnoDB a duplicate-key error <a href={`${mysql}innodb-error-handling.html`}>rolls back only the statement</a> and leaves the transaction open, with R11’s place still taken. If the application then sends <code>COMMIT</code>, the place is lost exactly as in Figure 1’s first option. The safe habit on every engine is the same: after an error you didn’t plan to recover from, send <code>ROLLBACK</code>.</p>

<h3 id="let-the-database-reject-invalid-states-where-it-can">Constraints</h3>
<p>A constraint is checked by the database for every writer, including administrative tools, imports and scripts that never pass through the booking code. Where a rule can be stated as a constraint, state it that way. Here the <code>CHECK</code> refuses a negative <code>remaining</code>, the unique constraint refuses Mina’s second booking in a class, the foreign keys refuse a booking for a class or customer that doesn’t exist, and the primary key refuses the same request twice.</p>
<p>A constraint also settles races between inserts. Checking “does Mina already have a booking here?” and then inserting can fail when two requests both check before either inserts: each sees no booking. A unique constraint does not have that gap. Suppose R10 has inserted Mina’s booking and not yet committed when R11 tries to insert the same pair. R11 <strong>waits</strong>: the database holds the statement back until R10’s transaction ends, because only then is it known whether the pair exists. Rows a transaction has written are <strong>locked</strong> until it ends, and another session that needs to change one of them, or to insert a clashing key, waits in the same way. When R10 commits, R11 fails with 23505. Had R10 rolled back, R11’s insert would have gone ahead.</p>
<p>A constraint error is an answer about the data, not a passing fault. “Mina already has a place in class 22” is true, and repeating the request will get the same answer, so report it. Later we meet errors of a different kind, which mean only that the transaction collided with another one. For those, repeating the transaction is the right response.</p>
<p>Two of our three rules can’t be written as constraints. A PostgreSQL <code>CHECK</code> sees only the row being written, so it can’t count bookings in another table, and it can’t look at other instructors. The article on constraints covers <a href={`${next}constraints/#account-for-rules-the-schema-cannot-express`}>the options for rules a schema cannot express</a>, and the article on changing data covers <a href={`${next}changing-data/#decide-what-should-happen-when-an-insert-meets-existing-data`}>what an insert should do when it meets existing data</a>, such as <code>ON CONFLICT</code>. For rules 1 and 3, the transactions themselves must keep the rule.</p>

<h2 id="two-correct-looking-transactions-can-make-an-incorrect-result">Lost updates</h2>
<p>The introduction showed the basic race: two sessions both read the stock before either writes, and both sell the last mug. Here it is for class 21. Mina, with request R8, and Tariq, with R9, both want the last place. The booking code reads <code>remaining</code>, and if it is above zero, works out the new value, writes it, and inserts the booking. Run alone, that code is correct.</p>
<p>Figure 2 draws the two requests as a <strong>history</strong>: one column, or lane, per session, with each statement on its own row in the order the database received it. A statement that has to wait stays on its row, marked as waiting, and its result appears on a later row, after whatever released it. A useful way to judge a history is to compare it with the <strong>serial orders</strong>, where the same transactions run one at a time. Mina then Tariq: Tariq reads 0 and declines. Tariq then Mina: the same, the other way round. Both serial orders give an acceptable outcome, and a concurrent history is safe if its result matches one of them.</p>
<p>Both requests run at <strong>Read Committed</strong>. How much one transaction sees of others’ work is set by its <strong>isolation level</strong>, and Read Committed is PostgreSQL’s default: each statement sees everything committed before that statement started. The first option is the race you have already seen. The last runs the same code at a stricter level, Repeatable Read, explained below. Before you play the other three, predict which of them keep class 21 at one booking, and how the request that loses finds out.</p>
<ScheduleFigure figure="race" number={2} caption="The first three options run at Read Committed, PostgreSQL’s default; the last runs at Repeatable Read. Between a read and its write, the application works out the new value from what it read; that code isn’t shown." />
<p>In the first option both transactions committed and class 21 ended with two bookings for one place: 0 remaining plus 2 booked against a capacity of 1. Tariq’s write replaced Mina’s with a value worked out from a read that was out of date by the time it was written. This is a <strong>lost update</strong>. No error was raised, because nothing in the database knew the value had come from an old read.</p>

<h3 id="the-protection-must-cover-the-decision-as-well-as-the-write">Conditional updates</h3>
<p>Both requests in Figure 2 already ran inside transactions. The transaction made each request’s two changes commit together, but it did nothing about the gap between reading <code>remaining</code> and writing it, and that gap is where Tariq read the same row. The protection has to cover the decision as well as the write.</p>
<p>The second option, <em>Subtract one, no condition</em>, lets the database do the arithmetic on whatever the row holds when the update runs. Tariq’s update waited for Mina’s transaction, because she had changed the row, then subtracted from her committed 0. The result, −1, broke the <code>CHECK</code>, so his statement failed with 23514. The rule held, but only because of the constraint, and Tariq’s application received an error rather than a clear answer. Without that <code>CHECK</code> the class would have been overbooked at −1.</p>
<p>The third option, <em>Change only if a place is left</em>, puts the decision and the change into one statement: <code>UPDATE … WHERE class_id = 21 AND remaining &gt; 0 RETURNING remaining</code>. At Read Committed, an update that reaches a row another transaction has changed waits for that transaction. If it commits, PostgreSQL <a href={`${pg}transaction-iso.html#XACT-READ-COMMITTED`}>checks the condition again</a> against the newly committed row. Tariq’s condition was then false, so no row changed and none came back. <code>RETURNING</code> is what lets the application tell the two answers apart: one row means a place was taken, no row means the class is full.</p>
<Prediction id="predict-rollback" title="What if Mina rolls back instead?" headingLevel={4}>
  <p>Use the third option. Mina’s conditional update has run and R8 is inserted, but not committed. Tariq’s identical update is waiting. Now Mina’s transaction rolls back instead of committing. What does Tariq’s statement return, and which bookings exist after he inserts R9 and commits?</p>
  <Fragment slot="answer">
    <p>Tariq gets the place. Once Mina’s transaction is gone, his update checks <code>remaining &gt; 0</code> against the row as it was before she started, which still says 1. It changes the row and returns 0. After his commit the bookings are R7 and R9, and class 21 has 0 remaining. A waiting statement does not decide anything until the transaction it waits for has ended, one way or the other.</p>
  </Fragment>
</Prediction>

<h3 id="a-repeatable-view-does-not-by-itself-prove-the-rule">Repeatable Read and write skew</h3>
<p>The fourth option in Figure 2 keeps the read-then-write code and runs it at <strong>Repeatable Read</strong>. Each transaction then sees the data as it was at its first statement, a fixed view called a <strong>snapshot</strong>, for as long as it runs. When Tariq’s update reached a row that had changed since his snapshot, PostgreSQL refused it with 40001, “could not serialize access due to concurrent update”. His application then had to start again from <code>BEGIN</code>. The new attempt read 0 and declined.</p>
<p>That refusal is PostgreSQL’s contract, not a property of the name. InnoDB’s default level is also called Repeatable Read, but there an <code>UPDATE</code> <a href={`${mysql}innodb-consistent-read.html`}>acts on the latest committed row</a> rather than the snapshot. According to MySQL’s documentation, the same steps would let Tariq’s write succeed with no error and lose Mina’s update.</p>
<p>Even in PostgreSQL, Repeatable Read only notices conflicts between transactions that change the same row, and rule 3 doesn’t give it one. Lena and Ravi both ask to stop teaching class 21 at the same moment. Each request counts the available instructors, sees two, decides one may leave, and marks its own instructor unavailable. Play Figure 3 with <em>Repeatable Read</em> and watch the rule check at the end. Its other options are repairs that come later; two of them use Serializable, PostgreSQL’s strictest level.</p>
<ScheduleFigure figure="instructors" number={3} caption="Only the instructors table is shown, because classes and reservations don’t change. The level each option uses is in its name; Lock class 21 first runs at Read Committed. In the two Serializable options, the second attempt after Ravi’s refused COMMIT is a new transaction on the same connection." />
<p>Both transactions committed and class 21 has nobody. Neither changed a row the other changed, so there was no conflict for Repeatable Read to refuse. In either serial order, the second request would have counted one and declined. Two decisions made from the same rows, each changing a different row, is called <strong>write skew</strong>. A stable view keeps each transaction’s reads consistent, but it does not stop two consistent reads leading to incompatible decisions. PostgreSQL’s Repeatable Read is <em>snapshot isolation</em>, and write skew is the outcome snapshot isolation allows.</p>

<h2 id="choose-protection-for-the-invariant-you-actually-have">Choosing a protection</h2>
<p>The figures so far point to a short rule of thumb: match the protection to the shape of the rule.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Choosing a protection from the shape of the rule"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Shape of the rule</th><th scope="col">Here</th><th scope="col">Protection that fits</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>One row changes relative to its own current value</td><td>Take a place if one is left</td><td>A conditional <code>UPDATE</code> with <code>RETURNING</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>A value must stay within limits in its own row</td><td><code>remaining</code> between 0 and capacity</td><td>A <code>CHECK</code> constraint</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A combination of values must not repeat</td><td>One booking per customer per class</td><td>A <code>UNIQUE</code> constraint</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A decision depends on several rows, or on a count</td><td>Class 21 keeps an instructor</td><td>A shared row that every writer locks before reading, or the Serializable level with whole-transaction retry, or a design that moves the rule into one row (all three below)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>

<h3 id="express-a-change-and-its-condition-together">The complete booking and version checks</h3>
<p>Here is the complete booking. It uses the conditional update from Figure 2, and it records the request before it takes the place. The reason for that order comes with lost replies, in Figure 5.</p>
<Sql code={booking} label="A booking that records the request, then decides and changes in one statement" />
<p>The application reads the outcome of each statement. A 23505 from the insert means Mina already has a place in class 21. One row from the update means she has the place, and no row means the class is full. A class that doesn’t exist also returns no row, so check separately if that difference matters.</p>
<p>The listing relies on three assumptions. The class is identified by its primary key, so the update can only ever touch one row. The update changes that row relative to its current value. And it runs at Read Committed in PostgreSQL, which checks the condition again after a wait. At Repeatable Read the same statement fails with 40001 instead, and other engines have their own rules, so don’t carry the listing across without checking.</p>
<p>Sometimes the decision needs code the database can’t run, such as a price calculation. Then a <strong>version check</strong> does the same job. Read the value, decide, and make the write conditional on the value still being the one you read. When Mina and Tariq both read 1, Tariq’s guarded update waits for Mina, and after she commits it changes no rows. Many designs keep a <code>version</code> column that every change increments, which makes the check independent of the business columns.</p>
<Sql code={versionCheck} label="Write only if the value read earlier is unchanged" />
<p>A failed version check means the decision was made from out-of-date data. The answer is to read again and decide again. Sending the same write a second time would just repeat the out-of-date decision.</p>

<h3 id="coordinate-decisions-that-span-several-rows">Rules that span several rows</h3>
<p>Rule 3 depends on two rows, and a conditional update only checks the row it changes. One repair is to make every change to class 21’s instructors meet at a single row first. Switch Figure 3 to <em>Lock class 21 first</em>. Each request starts with <code>SELECT … FOR UPDATE</code> on class 21’s row in <code>classes</code>, which locks the row: another transaction that tries to lock or change it waits until this one ends. Ravi’s request waits for that lock before it has counted anything. When it gets the lock, its count is a new statement, so at Read Committed it sees Lena’s commit and finds one instructor. It declines.</p>
<Sql code={leaveWithLock} label="Lena’s request, coordinating on class 21’s row" />
<p>The class row works as a <strong>guard row</strong> because it is stable: it exists whether or not anyone teaches the class, and every writer can find it. A <code>FOR UPDATE</code> lock lasts until the transaction ends, so it covers the count, the decision and the change. The protection only works if every path that can make an instructor unavailable takes part. An administrative screen or a nightly import that updates <code>instructors</code> without locking the class first can break the rule again, because the database only makes participants wait for each other.</p>
<p>Locking the rows you counted instead is not enough. A row lock covers a row that exists when the statement runs, and a new booking is a new row. If two requests each lock and count a class’s reservations, neither lock stops the other inserting a booking it never counted. The article on locks <a href={`${next}locking/#cover-every-change-that-could-invalidate-the-decision`}>runs that case</a> for a version of this example that counts bookings.</p>
<p>The second approach is to move the rule into one row. That is what <code>classes.remaining</code> does for rule 1: instead of counting bookings, each booking changes one counter, and a conditional update on one row is enough. Rule 3 could have the same treatment with an available-instructor count on the class row and a <code>CHECK</code> that it stays at 1 or more. The cost is that every writer must keep the counter in step with the rows it summarises.</p>
<p>The third approach is to let the database detect the problem, by running every one of these transactions at Serializable. That comes next. The article on locks also works through <a href={`${next}locking/#give-the-invariant-a-stable-coordination-row`}>complete guard-row protocols</a>, including moves, deletions and the choice of lock mode.</p>

<h2 id="understand-the-isolation-contract-behind-the-choice">Isolation levels</h2>
<p>An <strong>isolation level</strong> is the engine’s promise about which concurrent histories it allows. The SQL standard defines four levels by the anomalies each must prevent. Berenson and colleagues showed in 1995 that those definitions are ambiguous and miss lost updates and write skew, and engines have filled the gaps in different ways. So treat a level name as the name of one engine’s contract, and read that contract.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL has three distinct levels; asking for Read Uncommitted gives Read Committed. Here is what each allows, using the outcomes from this article. “Yes” means the anomaly can happen. “Refused” means PostgreSQL stops it by failing one of the transactions, which the application must then handle.</p>
<div class="cards" role="list" aria-label="Which anomalies each PostgreSQL 18 isolation level allows">
  {anomalies.map(a => <div class="card" role="listitem">
    <p class="card-name"><strong>{a.name}</strong></p>
    <p class="card-what">{a.what}</p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Read Committed</dt><dd>{a.rc}</dd>
      <dt>Repeatable Read</dt><dd>{a.rr}</dd>
      <dt>Serializable</dt><dd>{a.ser}</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>)}
</div>
<p>A set of transactions is <strong>serialisable</strong> if the result of running them together is the same as the result of running the committed ones one at a time, in some order. It does not mean the transactions didn’t overlap, and the order doesn’t have to match the order in which they committed. The article on isolation guarantees explains <a href={`${next}isolation/#a-serial-order-does-not-establish-a-real-time-order`}>why that order need not match real time</a>, which matters to a client that saw one commit before another.</p>

<h3 id="versions-and-locks-answer-different-questions">Snapshots, versions and locks</h3>
<p>PostgreSQL keeps more than one version of a row. An update writes a new version and leaves the old one for any transaction whose snapshot was taken before the update committed. This is <strong>multiversion concurrency control</strong>, or MVCC. Readers pick the version their snapshot allows, so a plain read never waits for a writer. Writers to the same row still conflict. A transaction that changes a row holds a lock on it until it ends, and another transaction that wants to change or lock that row waits.</p>
<p>A Read Committed transaction takes a new snapshot for each statement. Repeatable Read and Serializable take one at the first statement and keep it. Every level sees the transaction’s own uncommitted changes. Here is what one statement from Tariq does while Mina’s conditional update of class 21 is uncommitted, and after she commits:</p>
<div class="cards" role="list" aria-label="What Tariq’s statement does while Mina’s change is uncommitted and after she commits">
  {claims.visibility.map(row => <div class="card" role="listitem">
    <p class="card-name"><code>{row.statement}</code></p>
    <dl>
      <dt>While Mina’s change is uncommitted</dt><dd>{row.during}</dd>
      <dt>After she commits, at Read Committed</dt><dd>{row.readCommitted}</dd>
      <dt>After she commits, at Repeatable Read</dt><dd>{row.repeatableRead}</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>)}
</div>
<p>So a snapshot decides what a read can see, and a lock decides who has to wait. A snapshot does not reserve anything: holding an old view of class 21 doesn’t stop anyone else booking it.</p>
<p>Statement snapshots also matter to reports. Suppose a report at Read Committed reads class 21’s <code>remaining</code> as 1, Mina’s booking then commits, and the report’s next statement counts 1 booking. Added together, that looks like 2 against a capacity of 1, although no committed state was ever wrong: the two numbers came from different moments. Run at Repeatable Read, the same report reads 1 and 0, which agree. A report built from several statements needs one snapshot.</p>
<p>Old versions are not free. They take space until no snapshot needs them, and cleaning them up is ongoing work. The article on physical storage explains <a href={`${next}physical-storage/#updates-leave-work-behind-them`}>what that cleanup costs</a>.</p>

<h3 id="stronger-isolation-can-reject-a-result-instead-of-waiting">Serializable</h3>
<p>PostgreSQL’s Serializable level uses <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.4179">serializable snapshot isolation</a>. Each transaction runs on a snapshot as it would at Repeatable Read, and the database also records what each one read. Those records don’t make anyone wait. When it finds overlapping transactions whose reads and writes could not have happened in any serial order, it fails one of them with 40001, at a statement or at <code>COMMIT</code>.</p>
<p>Switch Figure 3 to <em>Serializable, repeat the transaction</em>. Nothing waits or fails until Ravi’s <code>COMMIT</code>, which is refused. His application then starts the whole transaction again, counts one instructor, and declines. The rule holds without a lock, and without anyone deciding which row to lock.</p>
<p>That convenience has three conditions. Every transaction that can affect the rule must run at Serializable: one Read Committed writer can break it unseen. The application must be ready to repeat any transaction, sometimes when no real conflict happened, because the tracking is conservative. And until a Serializable transaction commits, its reads <a href={`${pg}transaction-iso.html#XACT-SERIALIZABLE`}>may not be valid</a>, so don’t act on them outside the database before the commit succeeds.</p>

<h3 id="how-engines-differ">How engines differ</h3>
<p>The same level names describe different mechanisms in different engines, and the difference changes the code you write. The cards start with PostgreSQL’s three levels as the baseline, then show InnoDB and SQLite. The SQLite cards assume WAL mode, its write-ahead log setting, which lets readers carry on while one connection writes. A plain <code>BEGIN</code> in SQLite is deferred: it takes no lock until the transaction first reads or writes. Each card says where its evidence comes from.</p>
<div class="cards" role="list" aria-label="Isolation contracts by engine and setting">
  {contracts.map(c => <div class="card" role="listitem">
    <p class="card-name"><strong>{c.engine}</strong> · {c.setting}</p>
    <dl>
      <dt>What a read sees</dt><dd>{c.reads}</dd>
      <dt>When writes conflict</dt><dd>{c.conflict}</dd>
      <dt>What the application does</dt><dd>{c.application}</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="card-evidence">{c.evidence}</p>
  </div>)}
</div>

<h2 id="an-aborted-transaction-must-be-reconsidered-as-a-whole">Aborts and deadlocks</h2>
<p>Every protection in this article can stop a transaction. That is how it keeps the rule: preventing a bad commit sometimes means refusing good work. Each kind of stop means something different, so the application has to tell them apart. These are PostgreSQL’s codes; other engines have their own.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="How a transaction can end, and what the application does"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Outcome</th><th scope="col">What happened</th><th scope="col">What the application does</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{outcomes.map(([what, meaning, action]) => <tr><th scope="row">{what}</th><td>{meaning}</td><td>{action}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>In InnoDB, how much is undone depends on the error. A deadlock rolls back the whole transaction. A lock-wait timeout rolls back only the statement that timed out, unless the server runs with <code>innodb_rollback_on_timeout</code>, which makes it roll back the whole transaction. Sending <code>ROLLBACK</code> after an unexpected error gives the same starting point everywhere.</p>
<p>A <strong>deadlock</strong> needs two transactions that each hold a lock the other wants. Suppose R8 has taken class 21’s place, and Mina and Tariq agree to swap: Mina moves R8 to class 22 while Tariq moves R7 to class 21. Each move locks both class rows before it changes them. Play Figure 4 with <em>Each locks the class it leaves first</em>, then switch to <em>Both lock the lower class_id first</em>.</p>
<ScheduleFigure figure="swap" number={4} caption="R8 already holds class 21’s place when both moves start, at Read Committed. Each lane states which class rows its move has locked. A move’s last step frees one place, takes the other, moves the booking and commits, in one go." />
<p>In the first option each move held one class and waited for the other, and neither could ever finish. PostgreSQL checks for a cycle like this after a wait of <code>deadlock_timeout</code>, one second by default, and aborts one of the transactions with 40P01. In the recorded run it aborted Tariq’s, but the documentation says <a href={`${pg}explicit-locking.html#LOCKING-DEADLOCKS`}>which one it picks can’t be predicted</a>. In the second option Tariq’s move waited before it held anything, so no cycle could form. Mina’s move finished, then Tariq’s went through, and both class rules held. Here is Mina’s move in full:</p>
<Sql code={move} label="Moving R8 to class 22, with both class rows locked in a fixed order" />
<p>A real move also checks, after taking the locks, that R8 is still where the request expected it. A fixed order removes this cycle, not every deadlock, because foreign keys, unique indexes and other writers can add their own lock dependencies. The article on locks develops <a href={`${next}locking/#acquire-several-resources-in-a-consistent-order`}>lock ordering for moves</a> in full.</p>

<h3 id="retrying-one-statement-can-preserve-a-stale-decision">Retrying a transaction</h3>
<p>Switch Figure 3 to <em>Serializable, repeat only the write</em>. Ravi’s <code>COMMIT</code> is refused as before, but this time the application repeats only its write, in a new transaction. Nothing conflicts now, so it commits, and class 21 has nobody. The retry carried a decision made from the old count of two into a transaction that never counted.</p>
<p>A retry means running the whole operation again from <code>BEGIN</code>, including its reads and the code that decides from them. In practice the retry loop wraps the application function that reads and decides, not a list of SQL statements that a driver can replay. Retry only on errors that mean “try again”, such as 40001 and 40P01. Give up after a few attempts, waiting a short, slightly random time between them so that the same transactions don’t collide again, and report the failure. Nothing outside the database may happen inside the loop, or each attempt will repeat it.</p>
<p>Keep transactions short. Prepare input before <code>BEGIN</code>, and never wait for a person or another service while a transaction is open. A lock held during a network call makes every later booking for that class wait, and at Serializable a longer transaction is more likely to be refused.</p>
<p>Sometimes waiting is the wrong answer. <code>FOR UPDATE NOWAIT</code> asks not to wait at all: it fails at once with 55P03 if the row is locked. <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code> leaves locked rows out of the result. Neither means “full”; they mean the decision wasn’t made. The article on locks covers <a href={`${next}locking/#decide-what-waiting-and-skipping-mean-for-the-application`}>when to wait, fail or skip</a>.</p>
<p>Every booking for class 21 changes the same row, so bookings for a popular class take turns, however idle the rest of the database is. The article on concurrent load shows <a href={`${next}concurrent-load/#a-hot-row-can-limit-a-mostly-idle-database`}>how to recognise such a hot row</a>, and the article on request recovery covers <a href={`${next}request-recovery/#repeat-the-complete-logical-transaction-when-required`}>retry policy across a whole request</a>, including timeouts and limits.</p>

<h3 id="losing-the-reply-does-not-prove-rollback">A lost reply to COMMIT</h3>
<p>The booking service sends <code>COMMIT</code> for Mina’s R8, and the connection fails before the reply arrives. The service can’t tell whether the <code>COMMIT</code> reached the database, or, if it did, whether it has finished. Figure 5 puts the first attempt in one lane and the service’s repeat, on a new connection, in the other. Play <em>Book again</em>, then <em>Look up R8 first</em>.</p>
<ScheduleFigure figure="reply" number={5} caption="Both attempts run at Read Committed, and the lost connection is staged. The first two options use a booking that takes the place before recording R8; the last two use the listing’s order, which records R8 first." />
<p>Treating the failure as a rollback told Mina the class was full while the database held her booking. Looking R8 up first seems to fix that, but a missing R8 means only that the first attempt hasn’t committed so far. It may still be open on the server, with its <code>COMMIT</code> on the way, or even part-way through committing; until it finishes, no other session can see R8. In the second option the lookup found nothing, the repeat waited behind the first attempt’s change to class 21, and when R8 committed, the repeat found the class full: the same wrong answer.</p>
<p>The repair is to make the repeat meet the first attempt through the operation’s identity, here the <code>request_id</code>. The booking listing records R8 before it takes a place. Play <em>Record R8 first</em>: the repeat’s insert of R8 waits for the first attempt, just as R11 waited for R10 in the section on constraints, and the first attempt’s outcome settles the answer. If it commits, the insert fails with 23505 and the service tells Mina she is booked. If it rolls back, as in the last option, the insert goes ahead and the repeat books her, once. The primary key turns “did it commit?” into a wait with a definite answer.</p>
<p>Two limits remain. The repeat waits as long as the first attempt stays open, so bound that wait, for example with <code>lock_timeout</code>, and treat running out as “not known yet” rather than “failed”. And the identity only helps if every attempt at one operation carries the same <code>request_id</code>. The article on request recovery covers <a href={`${next}request-recovery/#resolve-an-outcome-that-the-caller-did-not-receive`}>resolving an outcome the caller never received</a>, including a bounded wait, and <a href={`${next}request-recovery/#make-the-operation-identity-part-of-the-data-contract`}>choosing and passing operation identities</a> through a service.</p>

<h2 id="commit-has-a-durability-boundary-and-a-system-boundary">Durability</h2>
<p>With PostgreSQL’s default <code>synchronous_commit = on</code>, a successful reply to <code>COMMIT</code> means the transaction’s record has reached durable storage in the write-ahead log, the file PostgreSQL records every change in before it changes the table files. The commit therefore survives a crash of the server, provided the disks really store data when PostgreSQL asks them to flush it; PostgreSQL’s notes on <a href={`${pg}wal-reliability.html`}>reliability</a> explain how drive caches can break that promise.</p>
<p>Turning <a href={`${pg}wal-async-commit.html`}>synchronous commit off</a> lets the reply come before that flush. A crash can then lose the most recent commits, up to about three times <code>wal_writer_delay</code> (600 ms with the default 200 ms setting), but the database stays consistent. That is different from turning off <code>fsync</code>, the setting that makes PostgreSQL force its writes to disk at all, which can corrupt the database in a crash. Synchronous commit can be changed for one transaction, so a low-value change can trade durability for speed while bookings keep the default.</p>
<p>That promise covers one server and its storage. Whether a replica has the change when a client is told it committed depends on the replication settings, which the article on replicas covers under <a href={`${next}replicas-and-partitions/#decide-what-an-acknowledgement-promises`}>what an acknowledgement promises</a>. Losing the server, or deleting a booking by mistake, needs another copy; that is the subject of <a href={`${next}backup-and-restore/`}>the article on backup and restore</a>.</p>

<h3 id="a-database-rollback-cannot-undo-an-external-effect">Emails and other external effects</h3>
<p>Suppose a booking also sends Mina a confirmation email. The database can roll back its own changes, but it can’t unsend an email, so where the email is sent decides what can go wrong:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Where to send a confirmation email, and what can go wrong"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Where the email is sent</th><th scope="col">What can go wrong</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Inside the transaction, before <code>COMMIT</code></th><td>The commit can fail or be refused, so Mina is told about a booking that doesn’t exist. The transaction also holds class 21’s lock while it waits for the mail server.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">After <code>COMMIT</code> returns</th><td>The process can stop between the commit and the email, so Mina is booked and never told. If the reply to <code>COMMIT</code> is lost, the service may not send it at all.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">From an outbox row written in the same transaction</th><td>The booking and the intention to send commit together. A separate worker sends the email and marks the row sent. If the worker stops after sending, the email can go twice, so the receiver should recognise R8.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The outbox works because it makes the intention part of the data the transaction protects. The article on background work develops <a href={`${next}background-work/#commit-the-business-change-and-the-intent-together`}>the outbox pattern</a>, and the article on connections covers <a href={`${next}connections/`}>how long a transaction and its connection should live</a>.</p>

<h2 id="check-the-argument-with-competing-sessions-and-failures">Testing a protection</h2>
<p>A protection rests on an argument you can write down: which rule it keeps, which writers can affect that rule, what each of them reads, and why the mechanism stops a bad outcome from committing. Test that argument with the histories that would break it. Open two connections and send their statements in the order a history gives, so the interleaving is chosen, not left to chance. Use a third connection to confirm that a statement is really waiting: PostgreSQL’s <code>pg_stat_activity</code> view shows <code>wait_event_type = 'Lock'</code> for it. A pause in your script proves nothing. Choose data that would show the failure: class 21 has one place precisely so that an overbooking is visible.</p>
<p>Random load is weaker evidence than it looks. In the recorded run, two clients sent at the same moment, with nothing lining them up, made the read-then-write code overbook every time, because both reads arrived before either write. On another machine, or across a network, the same code might pass a long load test. A load test tells you about timing on one system; chosen histories test the protocol.</p>
<p>Before relying on a protocol, write down:</p>
<ol class="checklist">
  <li>The rule, as a statement about committed rows.</li>
  <li>Every path that can write data the rule depends on, including imports, tools and background jobs.</li>
  <li>What each path reads, and what it assumes stays true until it commits.</li>
  <li>The protection, and why it covers that assumption for every path.</li>
  <li>What the application does with each way the transaction can end, including a lost reply.</li>
  <li>The histories that would break the rule if the protection were missing, run as tests.</li>
</ol>
<p>Run those tests again whenever a writer, a query, the isolation level or the engine changes.</p>

<h2 id="where-the-results-come-from">Where the results come from</h2>
<p>The five figures replay schedules recorded on PostgreSQL 18.6 on macOS (Apple silicon), with Read Committed as the default level, a one-second <code>deadlock_timeout</code>, no lock or statement timeout, and <code>synchronous_commit</code> and <code>fsync</code> on. A verification script ran each schedule in real sessions, confirmed each wait in <code>pg_stat_activity</code>, and compared every result, and the committed rows after every step, with the figure. The figures animate those recorded results; they don’t run a database. The script also checks the claims made outside the figures, such as the visibility cards, the rollback prediction, the locked rows that don’t cover a new booking, and the version check.</p>
<p>Figure 5’s lost connections are staged. The script closed the connection after sending <code>COMMIT</code>, held <code>COMMIT</code> back until a later step, or ended the session’s server process before <code>COMMIT</code> was sent. A separate check held a <code>COMMIT</code> that had reached the server, by naming a synchronous standby, a replica that must confirm each commit, that never connects. While that commit waited, the lookup found no R8, the take-first repeat waited and then found the class full, and the record-first repeat waited and then got 23505.</p>
<p>The load test raced customers 101 to 120 for class 21’s one place, in 20 rounds for each booking. In the recorded run the read-then-write booking accepted 392 of 400 attempts and overbooked in every round, and the conditional update accepted one per round. The two-client test overbooked 100 times in 100, and 98 in 100 in an earlier run on the same machine.</p>
<p>All 299 checks passed. <a href={sqliteUrl} download="verify-sqlite.py">A second script</a> checks the SQLite cards with Python’s standard library, and passed on SQLite 3.54.0; the MySQL entries come from its documentation. To rerun the checks, <a href={verifyUrl} download="verify.mjs">download the verification script</a> and <a href={modelUrl} download="model.mjs">its schedules</a> into one folder. They need Node 22 or later and a disposable PostgreSQL 18 server that accepts connections without a password, given with <code>--host</code> and <code>--port</code>. The script creates and drops its own database, and resets the standby setting it changes.</p>

